diff --git a/FRICTIONAL.md b/FRICTIONAL.md
index e4d997f..b1e7693 100644
--- a/FRICTIONAL.md
+++ b/FRICTIONAL.md
@@ -73,3 +73,85 @@ a bounded headless assertion, not a performance benchmark or visual review.
 It separates observable results from source-only architecture claims, requires
 visible normal-input collision and genuine wrap footage, and records the
 unresolved window blocker. No new recording or film is claimed.
+
+2026-09-27 benchmark session: Added godot/bench/ with four new files.
+
+bullets.gd change: const BULLET_COUNT replaced with var bullet_count: int = 500.
+Default is unchanged; a benchmark or test script may set it before the node
+enters the tree. The three existing tests still pass with 500 bullets:
+  MOUSE_FOLLOWS true BULLET_COUNT_500 true ACTUAL_POSITION (240.0, 164.0)
+  COLLISION_SAD true RECOVERY_HAPPY true
+  MOTION_OK true BODY_SYNC true ALIGNED_BODIES 500/500
+
+New files:
+  bench/bullets_nodes.gd  — node-based baseline: one Area2D + CollisionShape2D
+    + Sprite2D per bullet managed by a single _physics_process loop. Radius 8,
+    same SPEED_MIN/MAX, same spawn/wrap rules, collision_layer=1 mask=0.
+  bench/shower_nodes.tscn — same player script; area_shape_entered and
+    area_shape_exited connect to _on_body_shape_entered/_on_body_shape_exited
+    (Area2D is a Node2D subtype so the signatures are compatible).
+  bench/test_nodes_parity.gd — three checks against the node baseline:
+    bullet count equals requested count; one bullet moves left by speed x
+    elapsed physics time within 0.05 px over 60 frames; player.touching rises
+    when the mouse overlaps a bullet and returns to 0 when moved away.
+  bench/bench.gd — extends SceneTree; accepts --mode=servers|nodes --count=N
+    --frames=N; skips 120 warm-up frames; times each measured frame with
+    Time.get_ticks_usec(); prints one CSV header + data line plus two
+    Performance monitor lines.
+
+Parity test results (exit 0 on all runs):
+
+  count=500:
+    PASS: BULLET_COUNT=500
+    PASS: MOTION distance=64.058 expected=64.057 y_delta=0.000000
+    PASS: COLLISION touching=2
+    PASS: RECOVERY touching=0
+
+  count=5000:
+    PASS: BULLET_COUNT=5000
+    PASS: MOTION distance=33.604 expected=33.603 y_delta=0.000000
+    PASS: COLLISION touching=17
+    PASS: RECOVERY touching=0
+
+Benchmark results (godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path godot
+                    --script res://bench/bench.gd -- --mode=X --count=N
+                    --frames=600):
+
+  mode,count,frames,mean_ms,p95_ms,max_ms,object_count,node_count,static_memory_mb
+  servers,500,600,0.163,0.200,0.279,2000,6,25.00
+  servers,5000,600,2.671,6.454,37.737,6500,6,34.52
+  nodes,500,600,0.308,0.397,2.918,3501,1506,29.23
+  nodes,5000,600,12.579,20.864,87.578,21501,15006,78.30
+
+  Performance monitor on last measured frame (last frame only, not mean):
+    servers 500:  TIME_PROCESS=0.000 ms  TIME_PHYSICS_PROCESS=0.000 ms
+    servers 5000: TIME_PROCESS=9.953 ms  TIME_PHYSICS_PROCESS=33.452 ms
+    nodes   500:  TIME_PROCESS=0.000 ms  TIME_PHYSICS_PROCESS=0.000 ms
+    nodes   5000: TIME_PROCESS=0.015 ms  TIME_PHYSICS_PROCESS=55.449 ms
+
+What this measurement covers and excludes:
+  Covers: _physics_process (position updates, PhysicsServer2D body transforms
+  for servers; Area2D.position writes for nodes), broad-phase and narrow-phase
+  collision detection, signal dispatch overhead. The wall-clock sample around
+  each await physics_frame includes all engine work between physics ticks.
+  With --fixed-fps 60 headless the engine does not sleep between frames, so
+  the wall clock reflects actual CPU time.
+  Excludes: GPU work of any kind. bullets.gd draws all bullets with a single
+  _draw() / draw_texture loop; the node baseline draws via per-node Sprite2D.
+  Both _draw() paths are not executed in headless mode (dummy renderer).
+  Excludes: main-thread rendering, vsync, audio, OS windowing.
+  Summary: this is a physics-and-logic CPU benchmark only. The relative
+  rendering cost of one draw_texture loop vs N Sprite2D nodes is not captured
+  here and would require a non-headless profiling session to measure.
+
+Observations:
+  At 500 bullets the servers path is ~1.9x faster mean (0.163 vs 0.308 ms);
+  both fit in 16.67 ms with headroom. At 5000 bullets the gap widens to ~4.7x
+  (2.671 vs 12.579 ms mean); nodes exceeds the frame budget at p95 (20.864 ms)
+  and max (87.578 ms), while servers remains within budget at p95 (6.454 ms).
+  The node_count difference (6 vs 15006 at 5000 bullets) and memory difference
+  (34.52 MB vs 78.30 MB) show the structural overhead of the node approach.
+  TIME_PHYSICS_PROCESS on the last frame can exceed the mean due to scheduler
+  jitter; use mean_ms and p95_ms from the ticks sample for stable comparisons.
+  The claim that the servers approach is "a lot more efficient" is supported by
+  the 5000-bullet headless physics-CPU data. Rendering cost is unmeasured.
